BRICK (B) REGIME: EXACT Var/R2 census over SPREAD 11-sets (moments_exact, NO truncation)
  brick(A) delivers primitive diam>=16 => R2<=614; measure the true constant c=Var/R2 there
========================================================================================================
  [validate] block k=11 D3 = 0.404751  (THM-661 value 0.404751)

  sampled N=960 primitive 11-sets, diam in [16,44], R2<=614 (brick(B) regime)
  Var/R2 :  mean=5.769e-05  max=7.222e-05  min=4.724e-05
  D3     :  mean=0.7289  MIN=0.4917  max=0.8562
  bar(k=11) = 0.3312   ->  min D3 - bar = +0.1605
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WORST Var/R2 : c=7.2225e-05 R2=266 set=(0, 2, 3, 6, 12, 15, 18, 23, 26, 32, 36)
  MIN D3       : D3=0.4917 R2=550 c=6.425e-05 set=(0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 9, 10, 12, 13, 14, 16)
  block ref    : Var/R2=6.10e-5, D3=0.4048 (clustered EXTREME, R2=770 EXCLUDED by brickA)

  READING (brick B, EXACT):  over the spread brick(B) regime the DECISIVE floor D3 stays
  WELL ABOVE the honest bar 0.331 (min D3 - bar > 0), EVEN THOUGH the constant c=Var/R2
  reaches ~7.2e-5 (ABOVE the block's 6.1e-5).  So brick(B) [R2<=614 => D3>=bar] is
  EMPIRICALLY CONFIRMED, but its proof CANNOT go through a uniform c=6.1e-5 (that constant
  is NOT the sup) -- it must use that spread sets have LARGER E[W] (more uncovered mass),
  which lifts the PZ/degree-3 floor faster than the larger c lowers it.  E[W] vs R2 is the
  real lever, not c alone.  <-- key message for opus's uniform bound.
